15:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 9v4m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"The keys have to reach the remote Platform"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["The worker reads the ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Platform"}]," proto, not Bazel's execution info. Bazel\nbuilds the remote ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Platform"}]," from ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exec_properties"}]," (on the target or the\nexecution platform) and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"--remote_default_exec_properties"}],"; it does not copy\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"execution_requirements"}]," into it. If your actions never take the persistent\npath, set the same two keys as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exec_properties"}]," on the target, or pass\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"--remote_default_exec_properties=supports-workers=1"}]," for a whole build, and\ncheck the worker log for ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Spawned new persistent worker"}],"."]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","p",null,{"children":["Both wire formats are fully implemented. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"proto"}]," is length-delimited protobuf;\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"json"}]," is newline-delimited JSON with camelCase field names, where a request's\ninput digests are base64. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"proto"}]," is the default when the property is absent.\nAny other value is logged and the action falls back to ordinary one-shot\nexecution."]}]
17:["$","p",null,{"children":"The decision is made on the worker, per action, at the moment it is about to\nexecute. The scheduler only needs to know the keys exist; it does not treat\nthese actions differently."}]
18:["$","$L46",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process","children":"What makes two actions share a process"}]
19:["$","p",null,{"children":"Actions are pooled by a key derived from the command line:"}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"executable"}],": the first argument."]}]
1b:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"startup arguments"}],": every argument after the executable ",["$","em",null,{"children":["up to the\nfirst one beginning with ",["$","code",null,{"children":"@"}]]}],", which is Bazel's response-file convention.\nEverything from that ",["$","code",null,{"children":"@"}]," onward is per-request payload, sent in the\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"WorkRequest"}]," rather than used for pooling."]}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"wire format"}]," from ",["$","code",null,{"children":"requires-worker-protocol"}],"."]}]
1d:["$","p",null,{"children":["Two actions with the same three share a process; anything else gets its own.\nThis is why the argfile convention matters more than it looks: an action with\n",["$","strong",null,{"children":"no"}]," ",["$","code",null,{"children":"@argfile"}]," puts its entire command line into the key, so every action\nwith different arguments becomes its own worker and you get no reuse at all\nwhile everything still appears to work."]}]
1e:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":"$15:props:children:0:props:children"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"`entrypoint` shifts the key"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["If the worker has an ",["$","code",null,{"children":"entrypoint"}]," configured, it is prepended to the command\nbefore the key is derived, so the executable in the key is the entrypoint,\nnot your tool, and the tool name becomes a startup argument. It still pools\ncorrectly, but the key is not what you would predict from the rule."]}]]}]]}]
1f:["$","$L46",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-isolation-you-give-up","children":"The isolation you give up"}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":"This is the part to read twice. A persistent worker process outlives the\nactions it serves, so some of the isolation an ordinary action gets cannot\napply to it, and some of it did not apply until v1.7.3."}]
21:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Process namespaces, but not the mount namespace."}]," With ",["$","code",null,{"children":"use_namespaces"}],"\nthe worker process gets its own PID, user, IPC and UTS namespaces, as an\nordinary action does, so its children are reaped with it. It never gets a\nmount namespace or the private ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/tmp"}]," (",["$","code",null,{"children":"isolate_tmp"}],"): both are per action,\nand the process is not. Persistent workers see the host's ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/tmp"}]," and the\nwhole of the worker's filesystem, whatever ",["$","code",null,{"children":"use_mount_namespace"}]," says."]}]
22:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The action's environment, and only that."}]," The process is started with a\ncleared environment plus the command's ",["$","code",null,{"children":"environment_variables"}],", the way an\nordinary action is, and that environment is part of the worker's key, so two\nactions that differ in it never share a process. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"additional_environment"}]," on\nthe config, the side-channel sources and the per-action ",["$","code",null,{"children":"TMPDIR"}]," are not\napplied: they are per action, and the process is not."]}]
23:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"A shared, long-lived working directory."}]," The process's own working directory\nis the worker's root action directory, not the per-action one; it has to be,\nbecause per-action directories are deleted when their action finishes. The\nper-action directory is instead handed to the tool as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"sandbox_dir"}]," on each\nrequest, and ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"it is the tool's job to honor it"}],". A worker that writes relative\nto its own cwd will scribble into a directory shared with every other action it\never serves."]}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"No input digests."}]," The request carries an empty input list, so a tool cannot\nuse it to validate or invalidate its own cached state. Anything the tool caches\nacross requests, it caches on trust."]}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Measured, not enforced."}]," What the process and its children use while they\nserve a request is sampled and reported as the action's resource usage, so the\nsizing loop learns what the tool needs. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"resource_enforcement"}]," never kills a\npersistent worker for one request's reservation: the process is shared, and a\nkill would take every action queued behind it."]}]
26:["$","p",null,{"children":["The consequence is a real correctness requirement, not a footnote: ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"a\npersistent worker must be hermetic by construction, because nothing is enforcing\nit."}]," State that leaks between requests produces wrong outputs that are then\ncached as if they were right, which is the worst failure mode a build system\nhas. Only enable this for tools written for the worker protocol and tested under\nit."]}]
